      "text": "For pH scale, connect the macroscopic observation with ions in solution. Acids show acidic behaviour because of hydrogen ions in aqueous medium, while bases produce hydroxide ions in aqueous solution. Indicators provide visual evidence without requiring unsafe tasting or touching. In numerical or reasoning questions involving pH, remember that the formal definition uses the negative logarithm of hydrogen-ion concentration, while school-level questions often focus on the direction of acidity rather than detailed logarithmic calculation. Neutralisation is best understood through H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O. Carbonates and hydrogencarbonates are especially important because acid treatment releases carbon dioxide. Salt questions frequently test names, formulae, preparation, water of crystallisation and uses. The high-value salts include baking soda, washing soda, bleaching powder and plaster of Paris. For each, learn the formula, one preparation equation, one important property and two uses. The chlor-alkali process should be recalled as electrolysis of brine producing NaOH, Cl₂ and H₂. This expanded note is intended for board preparation: learn the principle, write the representative equation, identify the observation or product, and practise applying the same idea to a changed situation. Accuracy matters more than decorative wording. Chemical formulae, subscripts, coefficients, conditions and terminology should be checked before the final answer is written."
